{"causes":["The societal upheavals following the end of the Roman Republic created a fertile ground for philosophical inquiry, as citizens sought new frameworks to understand their world."],"effects":["Epicureanism's emphasis on reason and individualism gained widespread acceptance, shaping Western philosophy and influencing many prominent thinkers throughout history."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"lucretius","sourceName":"Lucretius","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"de-rerum-natura","targetName":"De rerum natura","context":"This poem is the culmination of his life's work, offering a comprehensive system of thought that reflects his intellectual curiosity and artistic skill."},{"sourceSlug":"rome","sourceName":"Rome","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"de-rerum-natura","targetName":"De rerum natura","context":"The Roman Republic's social and political upheavals sparked a renewed interest in philosophical inquiry, which De rerum natura responded to by offering a compelling vision of an ideal society."},{"sourceSlug":"epicureanism","sourceName":"Epicureanism","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"de-rerum-natura","targetName":"De rerum natura","context":"This poem is the central text of Epicurean philosophy, encapsulating the key ideas and principles that defined this influential school of thought."},{"sourceSlug":"classical literature","sourceName":"Classical Literature","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"de-rerum-natura","targetName":"De rerum natura","context":"As a foundational work of classical literature, De rerum natura has had a lasting impact on the literary canon and continues to be studied by scholars and readers today."},{"sourceSlug":"western philosophy","sourceName":"Western Philosophy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"de-rerum-natura","targetName":"De rerum natura","context":"The ideas presented in De rerum natura have shaped Western philosophical thought, influencing prominent thinkers such as René Descartes and John Locke."}],"places":[{"name":"Athenaeum","role":"Meeting place"},{"name":"Athens","role":"Birthplace of Epicureanism"},{"name":"Syria","role":"Place of exile for Lucretius"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: De rerum natura","type":"Reference article","slug":"de-rerum-natura"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q861986","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_rerum_natura"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"De rerum natura contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"De Rerum Natura ('On the Nature of Things') is the philosophical poem in six books by the Roman poet Titus Lucretius Carus (c. 99–55 BCE), written in dactylic hexameter and addressed to the aristocratic patron Gaius Memmius — the most systematic and complete surviving exposition of Epicurean philosophy in antiquity, and one of the most extraordinary works of Latin literature. In approximately 7,400 lines, Lucretius expounds the Epicurean atomic theory (derived from Democritus and Epicurus): that the universe consists entirely of void and atoms (infinitely small, indestructible particles of matter) in perpetual motion; that the gods exist but are entirely indifferent to human affairs; that the soul is material and mortal (dissolving with the body at death); that death is therefore nothing to fear; and that the purpose of philosophy is the achievement of ataraxia (tranquility of mind) through the rational understanding of nature and the liberation from the fear of death and divine punishment.

Lucretius wrote De Rerum Natura with passionate intellectual conviction and extraordinary poetic power — his poem opens with the famous invocation to Venus as the generative force of nature, and his arguments are illustrated with some of the most vivid and memorable passages in Latin poetry: the description of the Epicurean sage Epicurus breaking through the 'flaming walls of the world' to understand the nature of the cosmos; the meditation on the 'plague of Athens' (taken from Thucydides) that closes the poem; and the great analysis of human civilisation's development from primitive origins in Book 5, which anticipates the Enlightenment's secular account of cultural progress. The poem was apparently left unfinished at Lucretius's death (tradition says he died by suicide after being driven mad by a love potion).

De Rerum Natura was lost during the medieval period and recovered in 1417 by the humanist Poggio Bracciolini in a German monastery — the rediscovery described in Stephen Greenblatt's The Swerve (2011). Its influence on Renaissance and early modern thought — on Machiavelli (who copied the poem by hand), on Giordano Bruno's infinite universe, on Galileo's materialism, and on the Enlightenment's secular, materialist philosophy — was transformative: the text's ancient atomist, anti-theological materialism gave early modern natural philosophers a classical authority for their break with Aristotelian physics and Christian cosmology.","subjects":["Roman Philosophy","Classical Era","Epicureanism","Ancient Rome","Atomic Theory","Latin Literature","Materialism","Natural Philosophy"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"De rerum natura played a crucial role in shaping Western philosophy and continues to be studied by scholars and readers today.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
